ain.go exists but du_test.go is missing, while all other commands have their test files present. Looking at the complete file listing, I can see the pattern clearly—du is the only incomplete command in rel01.3.\n\nChecking the PRD for du, it references prd009-du with requirements R1 through R5. The main.go file covers the core functionality: recursive directory traversal with disk usage calculation, comprehensive flag support for output formatting and traversal options, hard-link deduplication during directory walks, and error handling throughout.\n\nNow I need to create a test file following the differential testing pattern used in the other command tests, comparing du's output against the standard GNU du implementation. There's a version mismatch in the status tracking—the code_status shows rel01.3 but implementation_status mentions rel02.0—but I should trust the actual source code state, which indicates the test suite spec is complete and ready for implementation. -depth, --block-size, --si, --apparent-size) all need coverage. For R3, I'm focusing on hard-link deduplication and directory traversal, while R4 handles error cases and exit codes, and R5 covers SIGPIPE. The test strategy is differential testing against the GNU reference binary `gdu`.\n\nGiven the 4-requirement limit per task, I need to group these strategically: default behavior and recursion together, flag handling as its own requirement, hard-link deduplication with error handling, and SIGPIPE handling separately. I'm hitting a constraint issue here—the PRD has 5 requirements but I can only map 4 to a single task. I need to either split this into two tasks or merge R4 and R5 since the SIGPIPE handling is more of an implementation detail than a testable requirement. Let me reconsider whether combining error handling and exit codes makes sense given the constraint. I'm adding R3 and R4 to round out the test coverage—traversal options with hard-link deduplication, plus error handling and exit codes. Now I need to structure the test file following the pattern from the existing tests: build the du binary, locate the gdu reference implementation, set up temporary directory structures, and run differential tests across various flag combinations.\n\nLooking at comparable test files, du_test.go will likely land in the 200-300 line range since it needs to construct directory hierarchies with files, symlinks, and hard links for fixtures, though the actual test logic can reuse the standard RunDiffTests pattern. The tricky part is that du's output depends on filesystem block sizes, which vary across systems—I need to figure out how to handle that for reliable differential testing.\n\nThe real challenge is ensuring both binaries operate on identical filesystem state. I should create temporary directory structures with known file sizes and test various flags like -s, -a, -c, -h, -k, -m, --max-depth, --block-size, --si, -0, and -b. Since both binaries will run against the same temp directories on the same filesystem, their block size calculations should match, making differential testing viable.\n\nI'm going to follow the same pattern as the existing cat and wc tests—set up a TestMain function to build both binaries, then create test cases that generate temp directory trees and pass them as arguments to both implementations. binary, locate reference binary `gdu` via exec.LookPath. I'll use pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests for stdout/stderr/exit-code comparison, and create filesystem fixtures in t.TempDir() so both binaries operate on identical state.\n\nI need to be careful here—these patterns come from existing codebase conventions and the differential testing architecture specified in the design docs, not invented decisions. Let me identify the key files I need to reference: the PRD at docs/specs/product-requirements/prd009-du.yaml for requirements R1-R4, the implementation in cmd/du/main.go, the reference test pattern in cmd/cat/cat_test.go, and the testutils API in pkg/testutils/difftest.go. Now I'm drafting the task with the title \"Implement du differential tests (prd009-du R1, R2, R3, R4)\" and creating cmd/du/du_test.go as the target file. I need to reconcile the metadata with what actually exists in the codebase. The code_status shows \"not started\" and no test files, but cmd/du/main.go is already there, so that metadata is outdated. The real gap is the missing du_test.go file, which I should create following the pattern of existing test files like cat_test.go and wc_test.go, keeping it focused within the 150-250 line target for a single task with up to 4 requirements.\n\nNow I'm checking whether there are earlier incomplete items—specifically, looking at rel00.0 and the magefiles task. The PRD mentions cmd/version as the only code generation target in prd011-magefiles, but it's not in the source_code, so I need to determine if that should be created first or if it's already handled elsewhere. So cmd/version is a valid generation target, but I need to check if there's a PRD for it—the analysis mentions an orphaned PRD but doesn't specifically reference cmd/version. Looking at the rel00.0 batch as foundational infrastructure, the magefiles use case points to cmd/version as a code generation target from prd011-magefiles, though I don't have that PRD content yet.\n\nThe constraint about external infrastructure complicates things—magefiles are provided by cobbler-scaffold and excluded, but cmd/version itself isn't part of magefiles, so it might still be in scope. Rather than get stuck on this, I should focus on what's clearly needed: the du test file for rel01.3. Let me step back and look at the priority ordering more systematically. For rel00.0, the magefiles use case is excluded since it's external, but format, sys, and testutils all have existing code and tests, so that batch is essentially complete.\n\nMoving through rel01.0 through rel01.2, the wc, cat, and sponge commands all have both their main implementations and test files already in place. But rel01.3's du command has the main.go file without a corresponding test file, making that the earliest incomplete work I need to address.\n\nLooking at the test structure from cat_test.go, I can see the pattern: a TestMain function that builds and locates the reference binary, then test functions organized by PRD sections using testutils.RunDiffTests with temporary files and directories. For du, I'll need to set up similar test groups covering default behavior like recursive directory traversal, size formatting flags like -h and -k, traversal options like -a and -s, and error handling for invalid paths, using gdu as the reference binary.\n\nNow I'm mapping these test groups to the actual requirements: R1 covers default recursive disk usage with a small directory tree, R2 handles all the output mode flags, R3 tests traversal behavior including symlinks and directory cycles, and R4 validates error handling for nonexistent paths and mixed valid/invalid inputs with proper exit codes. The acceptance criteria will be that du_test.go compiles and runs successfully against the reference implementation.\n\nFor the test structure, I'm following the pattern from the cat tests—building the binary in TestMain, locating the gdu reference binary, and using RunDiffTests to compare outputs across all the key flags like -a, -s, -c, -h, -k, -m, -b, -L, -0, -d, and the long-form options. I'll set up temporary directory trees with files, subdirectories, hard links, and symlinks so both binaries process identical test data.\n\nThe output format is straightforward—du prints SIZE\\tPATH\\n—and since both binaries see the same temp directory paths, the outputs should match exactly without needing a normalizer. The test file itself goes in the main package, same as the other command tests.\n\nSetting up the differential test, I need to create a temporary directory with a known structure, run both the reference and implementation binaries against it, and compare their outputs. The test will check for the gdu binary on PATH first, skipping if it's not available.\n\nWriting the test harness... I see that du_test.go doesn't exist in the source files, so I need to create it. Let me draft the task description with all the context the agent will need, including the PRD requirements, reference implementations from similar test files, and the testing utilities available. I need to continue describing the test requirements for the du command implementation. The next chunk covers the remaining flags from R2 (block size options, summarize, all, total, max-depth, null output), then R3 (hard links, symlinks with dereference flags, combined flags), and R4 (error handling and exit codes for nonexistent paths).\n\nContinuing with the remaining size and output flags like block size customization, summarize mode, and depth limiting, then covering symlink dereferencing behavior and hard link deduplication, and finally error cases where paths don't exist.\n\nFor the design decisions, I'm looking at how to structure the tests following the pattern from existing command tests—building the Go binary, locating the reference implementation, and using the test utilities framework to run differential comparisons. TestMain needs to compile the Go du binary and find the gdu reference binary, gracefully skipping tests if gdu isn't available. The test suite should cover a comprehensive set of flags including single-letter options like -a, -s, -c, -h, -k, -m, -b, -L, -0, -d, plus their long-form equivalents and special cases like the flag terminator, all validated through diff comparisons against gdu's output.\n\nI'm noticing a constraint conflict here—the requirements limit says 4 max, but the planning constitution targets 5-8 for code tasks. The explicit constraint takes precedence, so I need to stick with 4 requirements. For acceptance criteria though, the constitution suggests 5-8, so I should expand beyond what I have. I'm adding criteria around test organization with section headers matching the PRD, filesystem fixtures with nested directories, and hard-link deduplication testing to make the acceptance criteria more comprehensive.\n\nOn design decisions, I'll keep it at 3 since the constraint warns against inventing decisions not grounded in the PRD or architecture. Now I'm reconsidering the binary name—the architecture specifies using g-prefixed Homebrew binaries as reference on macOS rather than BSD built-ins, so I need to verify which binary is the actual reference for this task.\n\nSince `du` is a coreutils utility, it should follow the g-prefix convention like `gdu`, unlike `sponge` from moreutils which doesn't use that pattern. Let me estimate the test file line counts to ensure they're reasonable—TestMain around 28 lines, TestDU_DefaultBehavior roughly 40 lines with several test cases, TestDU_SizeFlags around 60 lines covering multiple flags, and TestDU_TraversalFlags approximately 50 lines for hard links and symlinks.\n\nWriting the test file now...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":75093,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":75093},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28acad09-86d9-437c-bb45-f99417e0eb9a","uuid":"0a8f4635-6748-44c9-b213-85e5156bcdea"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PAUJ7boMp8hxHj6qyEjk7W","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. This project derives specs from C source code, generates Go implementations via AI (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components: `cmd/` (utility implementations), `pkg/sys/` (platform syscall wrappers), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `magefiles/` (build targets, externally scaffolded).\n\n**Current Implementation State (from source_code):**\n- **Complete with code and tests:** pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format, cmd/wc, cmd/cat, cmd/sponge\n- **Implementation exists, tests missing:** cmd/du (main.go exists, no du_test.go)\n- **Not started:** rel02.0 (true, false, yes, basename, dirname), rel02.1 (tee), rel02.2 (head)\n\n**Current Release:** rel01.3 (du) is the earliest incomplete release. cmd/du/main.go exists but cmd/du/du_test.go does not.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nThe earliest incomplete release is **rel01.3** (Batch 1.3 — du specification and differential test). The implementation file `cmd/du/main.go` exists with full flag parsing and directory traversal, but no differential test file exists. Following P5 (dependency ordering: tests after implementation) and P1 (earliest incomplete release), the next task is to create `cmd/du/du_test.go`.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement du differential tests (prd009-du R1, R2, R3, R4)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd009-du.yaml (PRD requirements R1 through R4)\n      - cmd/du/main.go (implementation under test -- parseArgs, walkPath, formatSize, error handling)\n      - cmd/cat/cat_test.go (reference pattern for differential test file structure and TestMain)\n      - cmd/wc/wc_test.go (reference pattern for differential test file structure with named files)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (RunDiffTests API, DiffTest struct, NormalizeFunc type)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/du/du_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests for cmd/du against GNU reference binary gdu\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: |\n          Implement differential tests for default recursive disk usage behavior\n          (prd009-du R1). Test cases: single directory argument with subdirectories\n          and files, multiple directory arguments processed in order, single regular\n          file argument. Create a temporary directory tree with at least two levels\n          of subdirectories and multiple files of known content so both binaries\n          operate on identical filesystem state.\n      - id: R2\n        text: |\n          Implement differential tests for size mode and output format flags\n          (prd009-du R2). Test cases: -h (human-readable binary), --si\n          (human-readable SI), -k (kilobyte blocks, default), -m (megabyte blocks),\n          -b/--apparent-size, --block-size=N, -s/--summarize, -a/--all, -c/--total,\n          -d N/--max-depth=N, -0/--null.\n      - id: R3\n        text: |\n          Implement differential tests for traversal behavior (prd009-du R3). Test\n          cases: hard-link deduplication (create two hard links to the same file via\n          os.Link, verify size counted once), symlink handling with -L/--dereference\n          vs default -P behavior, combined short flags (-sh, -sc, -ah).\n      - id: R4\n        text: |\n          Implement differential tests for error handling and exit codes\n          (prd009-du R4). Test cases: nonexistent path returns exit code 1,\n          nonexistent path mixed with valid path returns exit code 1 but still\n          prints valid output, -- flag terminator with a filename that resembles\n          a flag (e.g., a file named \"-s\").\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          Follow TestMain pattern from cmd/cat/cat_test.go and cmd/wc/wc_test.go:\n          build Go binary with exec.Command(\"go\", \"build\", \"-o\", goBinaryPath, \".\"),\n          locate reference binary gdu via exec.LookPath(\"gdu\"), skip all tests with\n          os.Exit(0) if gdu is not found. Package declaration is \"package main\".\n          Use the same goBinaryPath/refBinaryPath package-level variable pattern.\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          Use pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests for all test functions. Each test function\n          creates a []testutils.DiffTest slice and passes goBinaryPath, refBinaryPath,\n          nil normalizers, and the test slice. No custom normalizers are needed\n          because both binaries operate on the same filesystem paths and produce\n          deterministic size output for the same files.\n      - id: D3\n        text: |\n          Create filesystem fixtures in t.TempDir() within each test function. Build\n          directory trees with os.MkdirAll, regular files with os.WriteFile, hard\n          links with os.Link, and symlinks with os.Symlink. Both binaries receive\n          the same absolute temp directory paths as arguments, so output paths match\n          exactly. Use known file content to ensure reproducible block allocation.\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: cmd/du/du_test.go compiles without errors via \"go vet ./cmd/du/...\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: |\n          All test functions use testutils.RunDiffTests for stdout, stderr, and\n          exit code comparison against the gdu reference binary\n      - id: AC3\n        text: |\n          TestMain builds the Go du binary and locates the gdu reference binary,\n          skipping gracefully with os.Exit(0) when gdu is not found on PATH\n      - id: AC4\n        text: |\n          Tests cover all short and long flag forms: -a/--all, -s/--summarize,\n          -c/--total, -h/--human-readable, --si, -k, -m, -b/--apparent-size,\n          -L/--dereference, -P, -0/--null, -d N/--max-depth=N, --block-size=N,\n          and -- flag terminator\n      - id: AC5\n        text: |\n          At least one test exercises hard-link deduplication by creating a hard\n          link with os.Link and verifying both binaries count the inode size once\n      - id: AC6\n        text: |\n          Test functions are organized by PRD requirement section (R1, R2, R3, R4)\n          with section-header comments matching the pattern in cmd/cat/cat_test.go\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":75093,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":75093},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28acad09-86d9-437c-bb45-f99417e0eb9a","uuid":"069aa4de-0b70-4017-8c1f-bf8e1bf8e899"}
